WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.136
-Our first guest tonight is
an Emmy-winning talk show host

00:00:03.136 --> 00:00:05.772
and actress you know from movies
such as "A League of Their Own"

00:00:05.772 --> 00:00:07.006
and "Sleepless in Seattle."

00:00:07.006 --> 00:00:09.509
She stars in the new series
"American Gigolo."

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:11.511
New episodes air Friday nights
on Showtime.

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:12.745
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.745 --> 00:00:14.413
-You said you talked to
that guy.

00:00:14.413 --> 00:00:16.282
-Finnegan. Kevin Finnegan.
-Right.

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:19.285
-Yeah, I talked to him.
-Okay. What did he say?

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:20.753
-Well, it was hard to tell
what he was saying.

00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:22.121
He was on his deathbed.

00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:23.957
He was nearly gone, you know.

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:26.159
I get in there and I said,
"Who hired you?

00:00:26.159 --> 00:00:28.961
Who hired you? Who hired you?
I'm talking about this case."

00:00:28.961 --> 00:00:33.933
And I don't know what, but he
started, like, mumbling stuff.

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:37.202
Didn't make a lot of sense.
Didn't sound like words either.

00:00:37.202 --> 00:00:39.338
But I thought maybe it would
make some sense to you.

00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:40.439
-What did he say?

00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:42.141
-He said Keene.

00:00:42.141 --> 00:00:45.111
-Please welcome back to the show
and to her former studio,

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:47.580
the one, the only
Rosie O'Donnell, everybody.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.549
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:57.023 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:57.023 --> 00:01:04.464 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:07.300
How are you?
-I'm well, how are you?

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.768
-I'm great. Do you feel it?

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:10.937
I know the physical space
is the same.

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:12.939
It looks so different.
-It looks very different.

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:15.475
But coming in here you get
the, like, "Whoa."

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:18.177
That was a quarter century ago,
right?

00:01:18.177 --> 00:01:20.612
-It was a quarter --
Yeah, I guess 20-plus years.

00:01:20.612 --> 00:01:22.948
And you did it I guess it was
about six, seven years

00:01:22.948 --> 00:01:24.349
you did your show?
-Six years.

00:01:24.349 --> 00:01:25.818
-Yeah. Doing it live.

00:01:25.818 --> 00:01:27.720
You were doing it live as well.
That's a whole nother thing.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.522
-And it kind of keeps you on
your toes though.

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:31.257
-Yeah.
-I didn't want some studio

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:33.726
executive to come and say,
"I didn't really enjoy

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:35.428
the Dom DeLuise cooking segment.

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:37.030
Could you do it again?"
-Right?

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.131
-And I was like, "Can't,
we're live. It's out there."

00:01:39.131 --> 00:01:41.700
-I want to ask real quick
because I was lucky enough to be

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.737
onstage presenting this award to
Sheryl Lee Ralph last night.

00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:47.640
-Fantastic.
-Which was really
a stunning moment.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.341
First of all,
well-deserved award.

00:01:49.341 --> 00:01:51.344
She's doing fantastic work
this year on television.

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:54.547
-Yes.
-It was scene stealing

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:57.183
on a night, where obviously, the
best in television are there.

00:01:57.183 --> 00:01:58.484
-You know, I've known her
since she was in

00:01:58.484 --> 00:02:00.185
the original cast of
"Dreamgirls."

00:02:00.185 --> 00:02:03.389
And on "The Flintstones,"
she played the adoption agency

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:05.190
that got the me Bamm-Bamm.
-Uh-huh.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.660
-So I've known her
for a long time.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:08.961
And she's a genius.

00:02:08.961 --> 00:02:11.764
She's an amazing artist,
an amazing woman.

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:14.834
And I was so proud of her, the
way she delivered her speech.

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:17.437
She sang from her heart.

00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:19.572
I don't know. I was crying at
home, watching it.

00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:22.208
-It was a really special thing
to be in the room for because

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:24.643
you realize in the way
that you've overlapped with her,

00:02:24.643 --> 00:02:26.913
you got the sense she's been
doing this for so long,

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:31.383
in so many different places
that she had probably just

00:02:31.383 --> 00:02:34.053
affected everybody's life
in there at some place,

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:35.754
and they were all so proud
and happy for her.

00:02:35.754 --> 00:02:37.657
-Yeah, and to see someone

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:39.425
be able to really live
in the moment.

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:41.894
Like, I don't know,
when I've won an award,

00:02:41.894 --> 00:02:43.763
I'm always sort of
out of my body.

00:02:43.763 --> 00:02:45.999
And I kind of like watch it
going, "Oh, my God.

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:47.834
I got to say something.
Try to be funny,

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:50.904
get to the backstage area,
hold in your stomach."

00:02:50.904 --> 00:02:53.539
I don't ever take the moment

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:55.875
to see somebody
just live authentically

00:02:55.875 --> 00:02:58.811
and just land there and go,
"This is what I'm feeling."

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:00.880
-It was really cool to see.
-It was beautiful, I agree.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.216
I loved the Emmys, by the way.
-I thought it was great.

00:03:03.216 --> 00:03:04.651
-I thought they were great
last night.

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:07.553
-Yeah, I thought they were,
you know, wrong on one or two.

00:03:07.553 --> 00:03:10.356
[ Laughter ]

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:11.758
I don't mean it.
I don't mean it.

00:03:11.758 --> 00:03:14.026
[ Laughter ]

00:03:14.026 --> 00:03:17.029
-I love that, though.
I love the faces when you can

00:03:17.029 --> 00:03:19.064
see the woman or the man
who doesn't win.

00:03:19.064 --> 00:03:21.767
It's just a millisecond of,
"Oh, crap."

00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:23.069
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:24.971
Well, that's when you go,
"You really didn't deserve

00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:26.772
because right now you're being
a terrible actor."

00:03:26.772 --> 00:03:29.675
-Exactly. Exactly.
-When they're like --

00:03:29.675 --> 00:03:31.610
-Yeah. Isn't that nice?

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.247
I was very happy for Jean Smart.
-Yeah.

00:03:34.247 --> 00:03:37.450
-That show "Hacks" is genius,
and she is astonishing.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.685
-Very similar -- someone who has
been around forever

00:03:39.685 --> 00:03:41.354
and just doing such great work.

00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:44.724
And then all of a sudden,
a couple of, you know, projects

00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:47.059
come that everybody's watching
and it felt like collectively

00:03:47.059 --> 00:03:49.895
they all said, "Oh, Jean Smart
has been amazing forever"

00:03:49.895 --> 00:03:51.396
and she finally gets
what she deserves.

00:03:51.396 --> 00:03:54.500
-She's just been funny nonstop
through her whole career.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.003
And she's doing dramatic roles
now.

00:03:57.003 --> 00:03:59.638
And this role, I think, fits her
like a glove.

00:03:59.638 --> 00:04:02.208
-Yeah.
-I just love the show.

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:04.410
I think it's absolutely epic.
-And it's a hard thing to do

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.345
when you play a comedian
because you have to be

00:04:06.345 --> 00:04:09.282
believably funny in a way --
-Which she totally is.

00:04:09.282 --> 00:04:11.351
-She really is.
-In the stand-up part, remember

00:04:11.351 --> 00:04:13.485
Sally Field did the stand-up
show "Punchline"?

00:04:13.485 --> 00:04:16.021
-Yes.
-It's a hard thing to learn.

00:04:16.021 --> 00:04:19.325
But because she's maybe
naturally a comedian,

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:21.261
she killed it.
-Mm-hmm.

00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:23.128
-She killed it
at the stand-up club.

00:04:23.128 --> 00:04:25.631
Like, you're watching, going,
"This is so believable."

00:04:25.631 --> 00:04:28.701
-I just read a wonderful
interview with you

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:31.270
in "The New Yorker" about
your early stand-up days,

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:32.905
that you decided to do that

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:37.042
as a path to find your way
onstage on Broadway.

00:04:37.042 --> 00:04:39.011
-Yes.
-And so what was it about

00:04:39.011 --> 00:04:41.180
stand-up that you thought might
be the first step there?

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.715
Because it does seem
a little unconventional.

00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:45.651
-Well, there was a kid in my
class named Craig Minervini,

00:04:45.651 --> 00:04:48.120
and his older brother Richie
was a stand-up comic.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.223
And he was older by
like 15 years.

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:53.659
So came to see our senior show,
where you make fun of everyone.

00:04:53.659 --> 00:04:56.829
And I was the star of it,
making fun of all the teachers.

00:04:56.829 --> 00:04:59.031
And he said, "You should be
a stand-up comic."

00:04:59.031 --> 00:05:01.868
And I said, "I really want to be
on Broadway

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:03.669
or a backup singer
for Bette Midler."

00:05:03.669 --> 00:05:05.571
[ Laughter ]

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:06.839
The fact that I can't sing

00:05:06.839 --> 00:05:09.575
didn't really stop me
from dreaming that.

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.477
-Bette Midler will take anybody.
She's not a perfectionist.

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:13.880
-She really will,
to be a Harlette.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:15.114
That's what her backup singers
were called.

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:17.383
-I remember.
-That was my goal in life

00:05:17.383 --> 00:05:19.218
and to get in a Broadway show.

00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:21.120
So I said to him,
"Richie, I don't want to do it."

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.055
And he said, "Well, come down
tomorrow night."

00:05:23.055 --> 00:05:24.657
It was a Saturday night

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:26.693
and all the kids
from my high school were there.

00:05:26.693 --> 00:05:29.262
And I had no material
because I was 16.

00:05:29.262 --> 00:05:30.963
And I'd say, "Oh, you know what?

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:33.565
Mary Lynn is dating Mitchell,
and Steve doesn't know."

00:05:33.565 --> 00:05:35.468
And the class of seniors
were like,

00:05:35.468 --> 00:05:37.870
"Oh, my God, that's so good!"

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:39.805
So I got like
a standing ovation,

00:05:39.805 --> 00:05:42.074
and the owner of the club,
Richie, comes over to me

00:05:42.074 --> 00:05:45.211
and says, "Rosie, why don't you
come back tomorrow?"

00:05:45.211 --> 00:05:46.746
And I thought,
"Uh, it's a school night.

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:49.414
I don't know. It's a Sunday.
My friends won't -- Okay."

00:05:49.414 --> 00:05:52.017
I went back and I killed.

00:05:52.017 --> 00:05:55.454
I mean, I bombed.
I mean, I bombed a death

00:05:55.454 --> 00:05:58.557
that could have been heard
in other countries far away.

00:05:58.557 --> 00:06:01.994
It was pure silence.
And I walked off and he said,

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:03.729
"So that's pretty much the life
of a comic.

00:06:03.729 --> 00:06:06.565
You kill it one night,
and you really suck the next.

00:06:06.565 --> 00:06:09.068
And why don't you come back
and be the emcee?"

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:10.535
-Oh, wow.
-And that's how I learned to do

00:06:10.535 --> 00:06:13.872
stand-up, by watching all those
comics for so many years

00:06:13.872 --> 00:06:15.842
and being the one
to introduce them

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:17.543
and then sit in the club
and watch them.

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:20.146
-That's fascinating because I
bet you were both learning

00:06:20.146 --> 00:06:23.516
how to be a comic by watching,
but I bet you were also learning

00:06:23.516 --> 00:06:26.185
host skills by being an emcee.
-100%.

00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:29.255
-Yeah. Because the host's job,
certainly those stand-up nights,

00:06:29.255 --> 00:06:31.957
is to make the room better
for the people who are going

00:06:31.957 --> 00:06:33.158
to come onstage.
-Exactly.

00:06:33.158 --> 00:06:34.327
-And I would imagine
you were great at that.

00:06:34.327 --> 00:06:36.295
-I really loved doing it
because I really loved

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:37.730
supporting the other acts.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:41.000
I really loved getting the crowd
ready and not doing too much

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.136
to spoil them for the headliner
or whatnot.

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:46.705
And then Shirley Hemphill
was the headliner.

00:06:46.705 --> 00:06:50.709
And she came in and saw me
on the open-mike night before.

00:06:50.709 --> 00:06:53.278
And she said to the club owner,
Richie,

00:06:53.278 --> 00:06:55.814
"You're going to pay her
$50 a show

00:06:55.814 --> 00:06:57.850
and she's going to open for me
this weekend."

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:01.019
He said, "No way, we pay her 20
bucks to emcee the whole night

00:07:01.019 --> 00:07:02.721
and we're not doing that."

00:07:02.721 --> 00:07:04.457
And she said,
"Then I'm walking."

00:07:04.457 --> 00:07:07.893
And she literally threatened
to walk out of the club.

00:07:07.893 --> 00:07:09.695
And he had to fold.

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:14.367
And he hired me and I made $300
in a weekend, when you're 16.

00:07:14.367 --> 00:07:16.501
I thought I was like
Leona Helmsley.

00:07:16.501 --> 00:07:20.038
I'm like, "Everybody --"
or Oprah, "You have a car.

00:07:20.038 --> 00:07:23.242
You have a car." You know?

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:24.810
-That's wonderful.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:27.980
[ Applause ]

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:29.715
Obviously you're back
in your old studio.

00:07:29.715 --> 00:07:32.918
You got to revisit another piece
of your old work.

00:07:32.918 --> 00:07:35.454
There's a wonderful "League of
Their Own" television show

00:07:35.454 --> 00:07:37.589
right now that you guys are
doing a cameo in.

00:07:37.589 --> 00:07:39.725
-Yes.
-We've talked in the past

00:07:39.725 --> 00:07:43.362
about the film itself,
which is really perfection.

00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:45.197
-It really is.
-It's so tight.

00:07:45.197 --> 00:07:48.768
And it's so well-crafted and the
performances are indelible.

00:07:50.035 --> 00:07:51.571
Did you have any hesitation
when you heard,

00:07:51.571 --> 00:07:53.672
"Oh, they're going to make
a series"?

00:07:53.672 --> 00:07:56.341
-Well, I met Abbi Jacobson
with Natasha Lyonne.

00:07:56.341 --> 00:07:58.611
I'm friends with Natasha.
We did a play together.

00:07:58.611 --> 00:08:01.948
And she's introduced me to
these 30-year-old kids

00:08:01.948 --> 00:08:04.116
who are unbelievably gifted.

00:08:04.116 --> 00:08:07.153
And so she brings Abbi
to dinner, and Abbi says,

00:08:07.153 --> 00:08:09.688
"I met with Penny" --
this is like 2018 --

00:08:09.688 --> 00:08:13.893
"and she said she would be very
supportive of us doing a show

00:08:13.893 --> 00:08:15.594
that 'League of Their Own'
is based on

00:08:15.594 --> 00:08:18.230
but looks more into
the sexuality

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:21.033
and the private lives
and the racism that occurred."

00:08:21.033 --> 00:08:23.669
And I remember thinking, "That's
going to be tough, right?"

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:25.938
But I didn't say that to her,
I said, "Well, all the support

00:08:25.938 --> 00:08:29.441
you need, if you ever need me
to play like a butchy bartender,

00:08:29.441 --> 00:08:31.210
just call me up, you know?"
[ Laughter ]

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:33.879
And so when the show was written
she called me up, she said,

00:08:33.879 --> 00:08:35.347
"Remember that
butchy bartender?"

00:08:35.347 --> 00:08:37.083
I said, "Yes, I do."

00:08:37.083 --> 00:08:38.751
She said,
"Will you come to Pittsburgh?"

00:08:38.751 --> 00:08:43.188
And I walked into the set
and here are all these

00:08:43.188 --> 00:08:45.790
30-year-old women,
like I was when I did it,

00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:47.927
wearing our uniforms,

00:08:47.927 --> 00:08:51.630
saying like the lines
in the vernacular of the 1940s.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:53.932
It was like I flashed back
all of a sudden.

00:08:53.932 --> 00:08:56.468
And I realized
that was half my life ago.

00:08:56.468 --> 00:08:58.236
-Yeah.
-Because I'm 60.

00:08:58.236 --> 00:09:00.906
And I was 30
when I was doing that show.

00:09:00.906 --> 00:09:02.942
And I just looked at them
and thought,

00:09:02.942 --> 00:09:05.945
"What an amazing 30 years
you guys have left."

00:09:05.945 --> 00:09:07.813
That was the start of my career.

00:09:07.813 --> 00:09:10.249
And this is the start of
many of their careers.

00:09:10.249 --> 00:09:13.819
And to look back at 60 and go,
"My God, what you have coming up

00:09:13.819 --> 00:09:15.421
is going to be thrilling."

00:09:15.421 --> 00:09:19.291
-It must also be thrilling for
you to notice in those 30 years,

00:09:19.291 --> 00:09:21.693
you know, sexuality was --
you could maybe say was

00:09:21.693 --> 00:09:23.395
some subtext
in the original film.

00:09:23.395 --> 00:09:25.130
-Not really.
-Barely, right?

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:27.332
-Yeah.
-But the fact that it gets to be

00:09:27.332 --> 00:09:30.602
so out in the open and the focus
of it now --

00:09:30.602 --> 00:09:33.606
I mean, I think you were
a huge part of the advancements

00:09:33.606 --> 00:09:34.940
in the last 30 years
but that must be

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:37.209
such a cool thing to see.
-Thank you very much.

00:09:37.209 --> 00:09:38.711
[ Cheers and applause ]
It's overwhelming.

00:09:38.711 --> 00:09:40.279
You know, it really is.

00:09:40.279 --> 00:09:42.714
When I watch the show,
I can't believe

00:09:42.714 --> 00:09:44.983
that we're able to have
representation.

00:09:44.983 --> 00:09:47.019
You know, when we did
the actual series,

00:09:47.019 --> 00:09:50.089
we would meet the original
members of the Peaches,

00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:52.625
and they would be like,
you know, 88

00:09:52.625 --> 00:09:55.694
and say,
"This is my roommate Betsy."

00:09:55.694 --> 00:09:57.730
I'm like, "Oh, hi, Betsy.

00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.598
Obviously you played shortstop."

00:09:59.598 --> 00:10:01.066
She was like, "Yeah, I did."

00:10:01.066 --> 00:10:03.602
I'm like, "Okay, and how long
have you guys been roommates?"

00:10:03.602 --> 00:10:04.970
"Oh, 52 years."

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:07.072
[ Laughter ]

00:10:07.072 --> 00:10:09.141
And it's just --
Society has changed so much.

00:10:09.141 --> 00:10:12.011
People don't realize, they say,
"Why didn't Penny add that?"

00:10:12.011 --> 00:10:15.681
Well, in 1991 when we were
shooting it, nobody was out.

00:10:15.681 --> 00:10:17.850
K.D. Lang wasn't out, right?

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:20.152
I mean, it was a different time.

00:10:20.152 --> 00:10:22.387
And I couldn't really believe

00:10:22.387 --> 00:10:23.855
where we have gone
in the 30 years.

00:10:23.855 --> 00:10:25.958
Although there's
a lot more to go

00:10:25.958 --> 00:10:28.961
and I think that representation
is the first step.

00:10:28.961 --> 00:10:32.130
And Abbi Jacobson hit it
out of the park, so to speak,

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:34.633
in this "League of Their Own"
thing. She really did.

00:10:34.633 --> 00:10:37.069
-Yeah,
she did an incredible job.

00:10:37.069 --> 00:10:39.972
And it was really wonderful
to see you in that.

00:10:39.972 --> 00:10:41.673
It's wonderful to have you
back here.

00:10:41.673 --> 00:10:42.942
Thank you so much, Rosie.
You're always welcome.

00:10:42.942 --> 00:10:44.376
-Thank you. Great to see you.
Any time.

00:10:44.376 --> 00:10:47.212
You're always welcome.
Rosie O'Donnell, everybody.

00:10:47.212 --> 00:10:49.581
New episodes of
"American Gigolo" air

00:10:49.581 --> 00:10:50.983
Friday nights on Showtime.

00:10:50.983 --> 00:10:52.618
We'll be right back
with Kevin Smith.

